          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines about eight times a month. Is that the level where guidance describes prevention, and what lifestyle changes are described?",
          "answer": "Eight migraines a month may be at the level where guidance considers preventive treatment. The national headache guidance (*Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits*) describes prevention for people with four or more migraine days a month who also have some disability. Fewer attacks can qualify if they are severe, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention. The guidance does not mention eight attacks specifically. Whether it applies to you depends on how much the attacks disrupt your life, and a clinician needs to make that judgment. Prevention aims to reduce attacks, not eliminate them, and a 50 percent drop in migraine days is the usual sign that treatment is working.\n\nOn lifestyle, the two sources differ. One retrieved sleep note (*Sleep and migraine: regularity, and the relationship running both ways*) says that changes in sleep, rather than the amount of sleep, are most often linked to attacks, and that keeping a regular sleep and wake time is the main point. The other search found no passage on lifestyle measures for migraine at all. I could not find any guidance on diet, triggers, hydration, stress, or headache diaries, so a general health clinician or headache specialist would need to cover those.\n\nThe guidance I retrieved does not report any disagreement between authorities on the threshold, and it does not say how strong the evidence behind the four-day figure is, so I cannot tell you how firmly it is supported.\n\nIf an attack comes on suddenly and is the worst headache you have ever had, or comes with weakness, numbness, confusion, trouble speaking, vision loss, or a stiff neck, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 15364.267330999837,
